nio: skip the old-task halt on the boot's first exec

Run 616 removed the kernel_task assert by halting the init task, but the
panel then stopped at load_init_program with no further progress:
task_complete_halt() waits for task->thread_count to drop to 1, and the
init task still holds the not-yet-scheduled cloneproc thread, so it blocks
forever.

The boot's first exec has no previous launchd task to tear down -- the
task is being created for the first time.  The halt sequence is only
meaningful when execing from a running user task.  Skip it when the exec
runs on the boot thread (current_task is kernel_task), which is exactly
the init/launchd case, so load_init_program can proceed.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/mach_loader.c b/bsd/kern/mach_loader.c
--- a/bsd/kern/mach_loader.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/mach_loader.c
@@ -669,6 +669,8 @@ load_machfile(
 	 * task is not yet running, and it makes no sense.
 	 */
-	if (in_exec) {
+	/* nio: boot's first exec has no old task to halt; halting here waits
+	 * on the unscheduled init thread forever. */
+	if (in_exec && current_task() != kernel_task) {
 		proc_t p = current_proc();
 		/*
 		 * Mark the task as halting and start the other
